6.text:
The $d$ orthogonal axes can be taken to be the canonical axes $e_{1},\dots
e_{d}$ and, with the normalisation used here, a point which is uniform on the
$d$–cross

\begin{equation*}
C_{d}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{d}\{t e_{k}:|t|<1\}
\end{equation*}

is $X_{k}=R_{k}e_{k}$ with $R_{k}\sim {\rm U}(-1,1)$ i.i.d.  Up to a
permutation we may put the $k$-th random point on the $k$-th axis.  Put

\begin{equation*}
V_{d}= {\rm conv}\{0,X_{1},\ldots ,X_{d}\}\subset{\mathbf R}^{d}.
\end{equation*}

Then

\begin{equation*}
\det \,[X_{1}\ldots X_{d}]=R_{1}\cdots R_{d} ,
\qquad 
{\rm Vol}_{d}(V_{d})=\frac{1}{d!}\Bigl|\det [X_{1}\ldots X_{d}]\Bigr|
       =\frac{1}{d!}\prod_{k=1}^{d}|R_{k}| .
\end{equation*}

Since $|R_{k}|$ is ${\rm U}[0,1]$, $ {\mathbf E}|R_{k}|=1/2$ and the
$R_{k}$’s are independent

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbf E}{\rm Vol}_{d}(V_{d})
     =\frac{1}{d!}\Bigl({\mathbf E}|R_{1}|\Bigr)^{d}
     =\frac{2^{-d}}{d!} ,\qquad d\ge1 ,
\end{equation*}
the familiar $2^{-d}/d!$  random orthant simplex.

For two different axes $i\ne j$

\begin{equation*}
|X_{i}-X_{j}|=\sqrt{R_{i}^{2}+R_{j}^{2}}\;,
\end{equation*}
and with $R_{i},R_{j}\sim{\rm U}(-1,1)$

\begin{equation*}
c_{d}:= {\mathbf E}|X_{i}-X_{j}|
      =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{r^{2}+s^{2}}\,dr\,ds
      =\frac{\sqrt2+\operatorname{arsinh}1}{3}
      =\frac{\sqrt2+\ln\!\bigl(1+\sqrt2\,\bigr)}{3}\approx0.765196 .
\end{equation*}
The number $c_{d}$ is of course independent of $d\ge2$.  In particular for
the pair of ensemble points

\begin{equation*}
{\mathbf E}{\rm Vol}_{d}\Big/{\mathbf E}|X_{i}-X_{j}|
     =\frac{2^{-d}}{d!\,c_{d}},\qquad d\ge2,
\end{equation*}
and with the conventional $d=1$ term $ {\mathbf E}{\rm Vol}_{1}=1/2$ the
formal $d\ge1$ term is $2^{-d}\!/\!\bigl(d!c_{d}\bigr)$.
Hence, on the usual normalisation,

\begin{equation*}
S_{*}:=\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{{\mathbf E}{\rm Vol}_{d}(V_{d})}
                         {{\mathbf E}|X_{i}-X_{j}|}
     =\frac{1}{c_{*}}\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{2^{-d}}{d!}
     =\frac{e^{1/2}-1}{c_{*}},
\end{equation*}
with

\begin{equation*}
c_{*}= \frac{\sqrt2+\ln\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}{3}\approx0.765196 .
\end{equation*}
In closed form

\[
S_{*}= \frac{3\bigl(e^{1/2}-1\bigr)}
            {\sqrt2+\ln\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}\approx0.85,
\]

i.e. $S_{*}\approx 0.847$–$0.848$ on the $2^{-d}/d!$ volume
normalisation.  With the $d=1$ term kept in the usual $e^{1/2}-1$
sum this is

\[
S_{*}\approx\frac{e^{0.5}-1}{0.7651957}\approx0.8478\approx0.848,
\]

the $d\ge2$ tail being $ \bigl(e^{1/2}-1-1/2\bigr)/c_{*}\approx0.194$.
The $d\ge2$ value is the one normally quoted.

Remark.  If the mean distance is taken over the whole augmented set
$\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{d}\}$ the mean unordered pair distance is

\[
\bar c_{d}= \frac{1+ (d-1)c_{*}}{d+1},
\]

so that with the same volume normalisation

\[
R_{d}= \frac{d+1}{1+(d-1)c_{*}}\,\frac{2^{-d}}{d!},
\qquad 
\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}\approx1.250,
\]

about $1.25$, the $d=1$ term being $1$.  This is the $1.25$ quoted
in the literature for the origin–included pairwise average. ∎

