5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The wording forces a bijection between points and segments. We have $d$ distinct mutually orthogonal open segments through the origin and exactly $d$ stochastic points to place, so up to relabelling

$$X_{i}=t_{i}e_{i},\qquad t_{i}\sim U[-1,1]\ {\rm i.i.d.},\qquad 1\le i\le d,$$

and ${\cal X}_{d}=\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{d}\}$. On this non-colliding realisation

$${\rm Vol}_{d}({\rm conv}{\cal X}_{d})=\frac{|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|}{d!}
      =\frac{\prod_{i=1}^{d}|t_{i}|}{d!}, $$

and with $E|t|=1/2$, $E|t|^{d}=2^{-d}$,

$$E_{d}{\rm Vol}= \frac{2^{-d}}{d!},\qquad E\|X_{i}\|=1/2,$$

the usual orthogonal simplex.  Collisions on the same axis are excluded by the distinct-segment condition; the $d!/d^{d}$ permutation probability is $1$ by construction. A positive probability of zero volume is therefore not part of the stated ensemble.

For $i\ne j$

$$\mu_{2}:=E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|
      =E\sqrt{t_{i}^{2}+t_{j}^{2}}
      =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}dxdy
      =\frac{\sqrt2+\log(1+\sqrt2)}{3}\approx0.765196, $$

independent of $d$.  With $N_{d}=\binom{d+1}{2}$ unordered pairs in the origin-augmented set

$$\bar D_{d}
   =\frac{dE|t|+\binom d2\mu_{2}}{N_{d}}
   =\frac{1+(d-1)\mu_{2}}{d+1}, $$

and the dimension-wise volume to mean-pair ratio is

$$R_{d}=\frac{E_{d}{\rm Vol}}{\bar D_{d}}
     =\frac{d+1}{1+(d-1)\mu_{2}}\;\frac{2^{-d}}{d!},\qquad R_{1}=1.$$

Hence

$$R_{2}=\frac{3}{8(1+\mu_{2})}\approx0.21244,\;
      R_{3}=\frac{4}{48[1+2\mu_{2}]}\approx0.03293,\;
      R_{4}\approx0.00395,\;
      R_{5}\approx0.00038,$$

$R_{d}=O(2^{-d}d!^{-1})$.  Summation is essentially exhausted after $d=3$,

$$S_{\infty}=\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}
   =\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{d+1}{d!\,2^{d}\,[1+(d-1)\mu_{2}]}
   =1+0.21244+0.03293+0.00395+0.00038+\cdots
   \approx1.2497\approx1.250,$$

to three decimals, $R_{1}=1$ being the one-dimensional contribution and $>85\%$ of the remainder coming from $d=2$.  In the $\mu_{2}$ normalisation this is $\mu_{2}^{-1}(e^{1/2}-1)\approx0.848$, the familiar $e^{1/2}-1$ form.

The $S\approx1.117$ values correspond to $X_{i}=R_{i}e_{K_{i}}$ with $K_{i}\sim{\rm Unif}\{1,\dots ,d\}$ i.i.d., i.e. sampling with replacement from the union $\cup_{k}[-1,1]e_{k}$.  That model allows $P\{K_{1},\dots ,K_{d}\ {\rm distinct}\}=d!/d^{d}<1$, gives $E_{d}{\rm Vol}=(2d)^{-d}$ and a positive mass of degenerate zero-volume hulls.  It is the i.i.d.-over-the-union, with-replacement reading, not the one-point-per-segment bijection implied by “distinct mutually orthogonal’’ and “number of points and line segments matching the dimension’’.  Conditioning on the intended non-degenerate simplex removes the $d!/d^{d}$ factor and restores $E_{d}{\rm Vol}=2^{-d}/d!$, i.e. the $1.25$ origin-inclusive sum above. ∎

15.text:
For the origin augmented set ${\cal X}_{d}=\{0,X_{1},\dots ,X_{d}\}$ put
up to a relabelling $X_{i}=t_{i}e_{i}$ with $t_{i}\sim U[-1,1]$ i.i.d.
and $E|t|=1/2$.  On the one point per axis, i.e. non collapsing,
realisation

\begin{equation*}
{\rm Vol}_{d}\!\left({\rm conv}{\cal X}_{d}\right)
  =\frac{|\det[X_{1}\dots X_{d}]|}{d!}
  =\frac{\prod_{i=1}^{d}|t_{i}|}{d!},
\end{equation*}

hence

\begin{equation*}
E_{d}V=\frac{(E|t|)^{d}}{d!}= \frac{2^{-d}}{d!},\qquad
E\|X_{i}\|=\frac12 .
\end{equation*}

For $i\ne j$ on different axes

\begin{equation*}
\mu_{2}:=E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|
      =E\sqrt{t_{i}^{2}+t_{j}^{2}}
      =\int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\sqrt{x^{2}+y^{2}}dxdy
      =\frac{\sqrt2+\log\!\left(1+\sqrt2\right)}{3}
      \approx 0.7651957,
\end{equation*}

independent of $d$.  With $N_{d}=\binom{d+1}{2}$ unordered pairs

\begin{equation*}
\bar D_{d}
   =\frac{dE|t|+\binom d2\mu_{2}}{N_{d}}
   =\frac{1+(d-1)\mu_{2}}{d+1},\qquad\bar D_{1}=1/2,
\end{equation*}

is the usual origin inclusive mean unordered pair distance.  The
dimension-wise volume to mean pair ratio in the $d!$ normalisation is
therefore

\begin{equation*}
R_{d}^{f}
   =\frac{E_{d}V}{\bar D_{d}}
   =\frac{d+1}{1+(d-1)\mu_{2}}\;\frac{2^{-d}}{d!},
   \qquad R_{1}^{f}=1,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $R_{d}^{f}=(d+1)2^{-d}/\{d![1+(d-1)\mu_{2}]\}$.  In particular

* $R_{1}^{f}=1.00000$,
* $R_{2}^{f}=3/[8(1+\mu_{2})]\approx0.21244$,
* $R_{3}^{f}=4/[48(1+2\mu_{2})]\approx0.03293$,
* $R_{4}^{f}\approx3.95\times10^{-3}$,
* $R_{5}^{f}\approx3.85\times10^{-4}$,

$R_{d}^{f}=O\!\left(2^{-d}d!^{-1}\right)$.  Hence

\begin{equation*}
S^{f}
   =\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}^{f}
   =\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{d+1}{d!\,2^{d}\,[1+(d-1)\mu_{2}]}
   =1+0.21244+0.03293+0.00395+0.00038+\cdots
   \approx1.2497,
\end{equation*}

i.e. $S^{f}\approx1.250$ to three figures, $R_{1}=1$ being the
one dimensional contribution and $>85\%$ of the $d\ge2$ tail being the
$d=2$ term.  In the $\mu_{2}$ normalised point–point form

\begin{equation*}
\frac1{\mu_{2}}\sum_{d\ge1}\frac{2^{-d}}{d!}
   =\frac{e^{1/2}-1}{\mu_{2}}\approx0.848,
\end{equation*}

which is the $e^{1/2}-1$ series quoted in the literature.  This is the
conventional non-colliding, one point per distinct orthogonal segment,
origin inclusive simplex sum.

Remark.  With the strict i.i.d. sampling $X_{i}=R_{i}e_{K_{i}}$,
$K_{i}\sim{\rm Unif}\{1,\dots ,d\}$,
${\mathbb P}\{K_{1}\dots K_{d}{\rm \;perm}\}=d!/d^{d}$,
$E_{d}V=(2d)^{-d}$ and $E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|=[2/3+(d-1)\mu_{2}]/d$.
The origin inclusive mean pair distance is then
$\bar D_{d}^{c}=[1+(d-1)E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|]/(d+1)$ and

\begin{equation*}
R_{d}^{c}
   =\frac{d+1}{1+(d-1)E\|X_{i}-X_{j}\|}\,(2d)^{-d},
\end{equation*}

$R_{1}^{c}=1$, $R_{2}^{c}\approx0.10927$, $R_{3}^{c}\approx0.00751$,
etc., so that

\begin{equation*}
S^{c}=\sum_{d\ge1}R_{d}^{c}
    \approx1+0.10927+0.00751+0.00038+\cdots\approx1.117,
\end{equation*}

the $d!/d^{d}$ collision penalised i.i.d. on the union value.
Removing the permutation penalty restores $E_{d}V=2^{-d}/d!$ and the
$1.250$ origin inclusive sum above. ∎

